fix(rpmh): read the IRQ registers at tcs_base, and decode the real TCS geometry

The IRQ_ENABLE/STATUS/CLEAR trio lives at tcs_base + 0x0/0x4/0x8, not at the
drv base: rpmh-rsc.c reaches them through read_tcs_reg() with tcs_id 0.  The
0126 probe read drv+0x00/0x04/0x08 instead, so those three numbers were not
the RSC interrupt state.  Corrected here: irq_en reads 0x00000001, i.e. only
TCS0's completion interrupt is enabled.

prnt_chld 0x800c8104 decodes to 8 TCS and 16 command slots per TCS for drv-2,
so the probe now walks TCS0-7 and all 16 slots.  TCS0 turns out to hold eight
leftover commands (two CLK at 0x30000/0x30010 plus six at 0x500xx), not two;
TCS1 has AMC enabled, trigger clear, enable 0 and every slot empty; TCS2-7 are
untouched.

The TCS1 writer is present as a compile-time-disabled draft under
KEBAB_RPMH_WRITES = 0: bounded readbacks, one request per boot, channel dead
after any failure.  It is not compiled into this kernel and has not been
compiler-checked yet, so it is a design for review, not a tested path.

--- a/bsd/dev/kebab_spmi.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/kebab_spmi.c
@@ -833,11 +833,19 @@
